<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Lutter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1626">1626</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Lutter am Barenberge · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Lutter was fought on 27 August 1626 near Lutter am Barenberge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> army under Count Tilly defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV of Denmark</a>, who had entered the war as leader of the Protestant cause in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The defeat led to imperial occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lubeck">Treaty of Lübeck</a> of 1629, which ended Danish intervention.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutter.jpg" alt="Battle of Lutter" width="320" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Caspar Lundorp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht_bei_Lutter_am_Barenberge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>As Duke of Holstein, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV of Denmark</a> belonged to the Lower Saxon Circle, whose Protestant members feared by 1625 that Emperor Ferdinand II meant to reclaim former Catholic lands in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In May 1625 Christian was made commander of the circle forces, and the Treaty of The Hague of December 1625 brought English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> subsidies. His plan for 1626 failed in part at once: Ernst von Mansfeld was beaten at Dessau Bridge in April, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-the-younger-of-brunswick">Christian of Brunswick</a>'s attack collapsed before he died of disease in June. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Albrecht von Wallenstein</a> drawn away to the Peasants' War in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a>, Christian believed he outnumbered Tilly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> in late July, Christian did not know that Tilly had received over 4,000 men from Wallenstein. Rain turned the roads to mud, and Tilly took the Protestant fortresses of Münden, Northeim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a>. Christian withdrew, his rearguard fighting off attacks for three days, and on 27 August made a stand behind the Neile and Hummecke rivers. Around midday Count Anholt seized the bridge. Taking a counter-attack for a general advance, Philipp von Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a> charged without orders and the whole line followed; Tilly's guns held fire until the attackers were 100 metres away. Fuchs von Bimbach and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> were killed, and after 17:00 Tilly attacked across the river and into the Danish rear.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Christian, who had four horses shot from under him, fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, leaving 3,000 dead or wounded; another 2,500 surrendered at Burg Lutter, while Tilly lost around 700. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> lost their whole artillery train and two wagons of gold. Christian wintered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stade">Stade</a>, abandoned by most of his German allies. Mansfeld, stranded in the Tatra Mountains with a starving army, died in November 1626. By October 1627 the Danes had been driven from the Empire, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lubeck">Treaty of Lübeck</a> ended the war in June 1629.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Lutter” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christian IV’s intervention in the Thirty Years’ War ended in defeat by Tilly at <b>Lutter am Barenberge</b> in 1626.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Christian IV won the Kalmar War of 1611–1613 but was beaten by Tilly at the <b>Battle of Lutter am Barenberge</b> in 1626 during the Thirty Years’ War, and the Torstenson War ended in the losses of the Treaty of Brömsebro in 1645.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Military</a></li></ul></section>
